From 4ea0e85c43a8a00243df1a5978ea9682a5a55c0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: milliluk-erik <build@localhost>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 21:42:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 47/49] protect: -protect-mode, physical code/data policy
 checks

The audit map (patch 37) records what a run DID; this enforces what it MAY
do.  One byte per flat physical address carries a policy -- "this byte is
code" -- and the CPU core's per-cycle access class, the same composition
the audit map stores, is checked against it in the CoCo 3 memory path.
Four checks, one flag each, -protect-mode for all four:

  -protect-mode-stack  an S-relative write lands on code
  -protect-mode-write  any other write lands on code
  -protect-mode-read   a data read lands on code
  -protect-mode-data   an instruction fetch lands on anything else

A violation reports the access -- physical address and symbol, registers,
access class, the flight-recorder ring when -trap-history armed one --
dumps flat RAM to -protect-dump (default protect-crash.ram), and exits 70,
distinct from EXIT_FAILURE so a harness can tell "the program under test
went wild" from "xroar was misconfigured".  The point is a failure at the
FIRST wrong access, with the machine state that caused it, instead of a
corrupted screen thirty seconds later.

An explicit per-check flag wins over -protect-mode from either side of it,
the rule -headless established for the options it implies.

The code/data map is inferred, never tagged in source: gensym format 2
classifies every emitted byte from the assembler LISTING -- an
instruction's bytes are code, an fcb table's are not, even when the table
sits between two routines -- and writes M C ranges that -symbols feeds to
the policy map.  Symbols cannot carry the distinction (a label is just an
address) and sections cannot either (a table inside a code section is
still data); the listing is the one build output that says it per byte.

Checks are dormant until the first instruction fetch from mapped code.
The CoCo 3 boot copies BASIC into RAM and runs it from physical addresses
no user map describes; arming at power-on faults on the ROM's own
housekeeping before the program under test exists.  First fetch of mapped
code is the moment it exists.  -trap-protect-on / -trap-protect-off
rescope explicitly when that default is wrong -- including the program
that calls back into BASIC, which should expect to need them (or
-no-protect-mode-data).

Wired exactly where the audit map is wired -- coco3.c, both MMU branches,
read and write -- because that is the one place the physical address from
the GIME's own decode and the access class from the core meet.  Fatal when
armed on any other machine, or with no M records loaded: a protect run
that silently protects nothing is worse than one that refuses to start.

Self-modifying code trips -protect-mode-write by design; -protect-allow
LO-HI exempts the patched bytes, with @names resolving there like any
other address.  Code the build cannot see (copied to RAM at runtime) needs
the same, or its own M range.

The stack check as first written fired only when an S-derived write landed
on CODE, which is the rarest of the ways a stack goes wrong.  A program
that points S into an fcb table and pushes, and a program whose stack
simply grows past the bottom of the space reserved for it, both ran to
completion and exited 0 with -protect-mode-stack armed.  Two additions
close that, and they are deliberately separate, because the two failures
land in different kinds of memory:

  M D records.  gensym already classified every listing line as
  instruction, data directive or unknown, and emitted only the code ranges;
  it now emits the data-directive ranges too, and an S-derived write onto
  one is a fault.  The class is INITIALIZED data by construction: rmb and
  the other reservations emit no bytes into an lwasm listing, so the
  classifier never sees them and no M D range can cover them.  That is what
  keeps a program's own stack, buffers and scratch -- all of which live in
  rmb space -- out of the data class, and it is the whole reason this does
  not false-positive.  An ordinary write to declared data (`sta Scratch`
  where `Scratch fcb 0`) is normal 6809 and still must not fault, so only
  the S-derived write consults the data class; -protect-mode-write is
  unchanged and still means code only.  symfile takes C and D and still
  rejects any other class loudly; a format-2 file from before this, with
  only C records, loads unchanged.

  -protect-stack-floor ADDR.  A physical floor, with @names resolving as
  they do for -protect-allow.  It catches what M D cannot see: a stack
  growing down through the rmb space below it, where by the property above
  there is deliberately nothing to hit.  No build output states how far
  down a stack may go, so this is the one number that has to be given
  rather than inferred, and it is unset by default.

The report names which of the three fired -- "stack hit code", "stack hit
data", "stack below floor (0xNNNNN)" -- because that is most of the
diagnosis and the verdict itself is a single bit.

One hazard, stated rather than hidden: AUDIT_STACK_S is *any* S-derived
address, including the classic 6809 trick of using pshs/puls or S-relative
indexing as a fast data pointer.  A program that deliberately pushes into
an initialized data area will now fault, correctly by the rule and wrongly
by intent.  -protect-allow LO-HI is the escape hatch, and protect.h says so
at the point where someone hitting it will look.

One more thing the first version got wrong, and it is the option that
advertised the behaviour: -trap-protect-on could not defer arming past
boot, which is the only thing anyone would reach for it to do.  Arming is
automatic at the first instruction fetch from mapped code, and the only
thing that cleared that automatic arming was protect_set_active(), which
runs when the trap FIRES.  By then the program's own entry has already
fetched mapped code and the checks have been live for millions of cycles,
so the option re-armed what was already armed and nothing was ever
scoped.  A run that says "check nothing until @ToolEntry" got checks from
the first instruction, and the only evidence was a fault the user thought
they had excluded.

The presence of the option now turns the automatic arming off while the
command line is being applied (protect_defer_arming(), called from the
trap scan beside protect_arm()), so the trap really is where the checks
begin.  -trap-protect-off alone still does NOT defer: that pairing means
"arm as usual, stop checking here", and treating it the same way would
leave a run with no checks at all.

That scan runs BEFORE protect_arm(), and the ordering is not incidental.
protect_arm() logs the line an operator reads to confirm the flag took
effect -- "armed (checks 0x..): N code ranges, ... dormant until first
code fetch" -- and it composes that line from protect_auto.  Deferring
after it therefore left a run that really did defer announcing that it
would arm at the first code fetch: right behaviour, wrong evidence, and
the evidence is the only part anyone can see.  A log line that
contradicts what the emulator is doing is worse than either being wrong
on its own, because it sends the reader to debug the wrong half.

Same rule applied to the floor in that line: an unset floor prints
"none", not "0x00000", which reads as a floor at address zero.
